{
  "name": "happy",
  "integration": "claroty-ctd",
  "description": "One page of the real CTD asset shapes: a multi-IP OT host (68-1), an IT endpoint with a real hostname and an OS (15-1), a MAC-only L2 device whose name is its own MAC (79-1), and a Rockwell backplane card (33-1) that carries neither an IPv4 nor a MAC, only a bus address. 68-1 also carries 33-1 as a full nested children[] object, which is the double-count trap: 33-1 is ALSO its own top-level row, so the run must emit four assets rather than five and must record the child only as a relationship attribute. A ghost row and a multicast pseudo-asset are dropped. The declared matchBehavior is asserted from the run log rather than from _match.behavior, which the export only carries when id matching is disabled. has_correlator is skipped because 33-1 is genuinely address-less on a real appliance - a backplane card is addressed by slot - and dropping it would lose the OT hardware detail this source exists to provide; it still reconciles poll to poll on its foreign id. plc_slots carries the REAL recorded nesting -- each entry's only key is PLCSlotInformation, whose value holds a PLCInformation object -- rather than the flat card record an earlier revision invented and then asserted. Slots 0 and 5 also pin that the attribute is keyed on the card's own slot number: keyed on the array index, slot 5 would be reported as plc_slot_1.",
